    public static int maxProfit(int[] prices) {
        
        // Approach:
        // - Instead of checking every possible buy-sell pair, keep track of the
        //   minimum price encountered so far while traversing the array.
        // - For each day's price:
        //      1. Update the minimum buying price if the current price is smaller.
        //      2. Calculate the profit by selling on the current day
        //         (current price - minimum price so far).
        //      3. Update the maximum profit if the current profit is greater.
        // - This allows us to find the maximum profit in a single traversal of the array.
        
        // Time Complexity: O(n)
        // - The array is traversed only once.
        // - Each iteration performs constant-time operations:
        //      • Compare current price with minimum price.
        //      • Calculate the current profit.
        //      • Update the maximum profit using Math.max().
        // - Since each element is visited exactly once, the time complexity is O(n).
        
        // Space Complexity: O(1)
        // - No extra data structures (arrays, lists, maps, etc.) are used.
        // - Only two integer variables (min and profit) are maintained
        //   regardless of the input size.
        // - Therefore, the space complexity is constant.
        
        int min = prices[0];   // Stores the minimum buying price seen so far.
        int profit = 0;        // Stores the maximum profit found so far.
        
        // Traverse through each day's stock price.
        for (int i = 0; i < prices.length; i++) {
        
            // If the current price is lower than the minimum price seen so far,
            // update the minimum buying price.
            if (prices[i] < min)
                min = prices[i];
        
            // Calculate the profit if we sell on the current day after buying
            // at the minimum price seen so far.
            // Update the maximum profit if the current profit is greater.
            // profit = Math.max(profit, prices[i] - min);
            if(prices[i] - min > profit)
                profit = prices[i] - min;
        }
        
        // Return the maximum profit.
        // If no profitable transaction is possible, the value remains 0.
        return profit;
        
        

